{
  "passage": "A simple electric circuit needs three things: a source of electricity such as a cell, a device that uses electricity such as a bulb, and connecting wires that make a complete path. Current flows only when the path is closed. A switch is used to open or close this path. When the switch is on, the circuit is closed and the bulb glows. When it is off, there is a gap in the path and the current stops. A cell has two terminals, a positive terminal and a negative terminal. In a circuit diagram, the longer line stands for the positive terminal. When two or more cells are joined, the positive terminal of one cell must be connected to the negative terminal of the next. This arrangement is called a battery. Bulbs can also be joined one after another in a single path. This is called a series connection. In a series circuit the same current passes through every bulb, and the current is not used up on the way: just as much current returns to the cell as leaves it. However, if one bulb in a series circuit fuses, the path is broken and all the other bulbs go off too. In our homes, lights and fans are not joined in series. They are joined in parallel, so that each one can be switched on or off without affecting the others.",
